          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "If someone has a raised blood pressure reading but no diagnosis of hypertension, which chapter does that belong in?",
          "answer": "A raised blood pressure reading with no hypertension diagnosis is classified in **Chapter 18, Symptoms, signs and abnormal findings (R00–R99)**. The passages place it in the R00–R09 block, which covers circulatory and respiratory symptoms and signs. They do not confirm the exact three-character code, so check that against the official code set.\n\nThe rule that decides this is documentation, not the number. A single high reading, or several, is treated as a finding until a clinician records a hypertension diagnosis. Only then does the case move to the hypertension codes in Chapter 9 (I10–I16). The coding summary states this directly: \"the diagnosis in the record, not the number, is what moves a case to I10.\" The same logic applies generally in Chapter 18: once a diagnosis is documented, the diagnosis code replaces the symptom or finding code.\n\nSources: \"Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family\"; \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 18: symptoms, signs and abnormal findings (R00-R99)\"; \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 9: diseases of the circulatory system (I00-I99).\" These are educational summaries, not a coding manual. For a real medical record, a clinician or certified coder should assign the code.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
